  "query_preview": "..Utah and southwest Wyoming remains under generally\ndry southwest flow today behind a transient ridge, keeping\nconvective chances at bay areawide. More active weather returns \non Sunday as an anomalous upper-level low begins to dig southward \nalong the west coast. As southerly flow increases aloft ahead of\nthis system, gusty winds are expected to develop across the\nwestern half of Utah, with widespread gusts 25-30 mph. Mid-level \nmoisture will begin to draw up and into the region on Sunday as \nwell, yielding the development of widely scattered showers and \nthunderstorms Sunday afternoon and evening, with greatest coverage\nfavored along and east of the topographic spine of Utah. Main \nthreat with these storms include gusty and erratic outflow winds, \nwith gusts in excess of 40 mph possible. Convective activity will\ntaper off overnight, though some isolated showers may linger over\nthe higher terrain into Monday morning.\n\nModels are in good agreement that the aforementioned upper-level\nlow will eject eastward across the Desert Southwest on Monday,\nbringing ample instability to the region as the associated vort\nmax traverses the region late Monday into early Tuesday. This\nsystem will draw even deeper moisture into the region as it taps\ninto the reserves of Hurricane Odalys and Polo as they spin off\nthe coast of the Baja Peninsula. Ensembles show strong agreement\nin this influx of tropical moisture resulting in widespread\nprecipitation across most of the region on Monday. They also have\na strong consensus that deepest moisture will be favored across\nsoutheast Utah given the track of the upper-level low. However,\nlike many setups where tropical systems are involved, a good\namount of uncertainty still exists regarding the exact impacts\nthis precipitation will bring to our area. Previous model runs\nfavored a heavy rainfall and associated flash flood risk across\nsoutheast Utah on Monday afternoon. However, recent runs show \nmore stratiform rain across southeast Utah due to considerable\ncloud cover, with a favorable environment for heavy rainfall \nacross southwest Utah on Monday afternoon. Which solution comes to\nfruition will be heavily dependent on the track of both the\ntropical systems and the upper-level low. Regardless, expect\nwidespread wetting rains across central and southern Utah, with\nthe 25th percentile amounts between Monday and Tuesday between\n0.5-1.00\" across much of the region. Upper-end scenarios bring as\nmuch as 1.25-1.5\" of rain to much of central and southern Utah.\n\nAs the upper-level low continues to traverse into Arizona on\nTuesday, ample instability and moisture will exist across far \nsouthern Utah on Tuesday for additional convective activity to \nredevelop during the afternoon hours. A drier airmass quickly\nfills in across the region on Wednesday, cutting off any lingering\nactivity across southern Utah by Wednesday morning. This dry\nairmass will prevail through the remainder of the work week as\nhigh pressure develops across the west, keeping conditions\nseasonable and benign through this period.",
